   195→\n   196→        .insight-type.tutorial {\n   197→            background-color: #f0e6ff;\n   198→            color: #4a0080;\n   199→        }\n   200→\n   201→        .quote {\n   202→            font-style: italic;\n   203→            color: #828282;\n   204→            font-size: 8pt;\n   205→            margin-top: 4px;\n   206→            padding-left: 10px;\n   207→            border-left: 2px solid #d4d4d4;\n   208→        }\n   209→\n   210→        footer {\n   211→            margin-top: 20px;\n   212→            padding: 10px;\n   213→            text-align: center;\n   214→            font-size: 9pt;\n   215→            color: #828282;\n   216→            border-top: 1px solid #d4d4d4;\n   217→        }\n   218→\n   219→        footer a {\n   220→            color: #828282;\n   221→            text-decoration: none;\n   222→        }\n   223→\n   224→        footer a:hover {\n   225→            color: #ff6600;\n   226→        }\n   227→\n   228→        .source-list {\n   229→            display: inline;\n   230→        }\n   231→\n   232→        .source-list::before {\n   233→            content: \"(\";\n   234→        }\n   235→\n   236→        .source-list::after {\n   237→            content: \")\";\n   238→        }\n   239→\n   240→        @media print {\n   241→            body {\n   242→                background-color: #ffffff;\n   243→            }\n   244→\n   245→            section {\n   246→                border: 1px solid #000000;\n   247→                page-break-inside: avoid;\n   248→            }\n   249→\n   250→            a {\n   251→                color: #000000;\n   252→                text-decoration: underline;\n   253→            }\n   254→        }\n   255→    </style>\n   256→</head>\n   257→<body>\n   258→    <header>\n   259→        <div class=\"header-content\">\n   260→            <div class=\"header-title\">Agentic Coding Research Report</div>\n   261→            <div class=\"header-date\">April 30, 2026</div>\n   262→        </div>\n   263→    </header>\n   264→\n   265→    <div class=\"header-nav\">\n   266→        <a href=\"#new-products\">New Products</a>\n   267→        <a href=\"#whitelist-updates\">Whitelist Updates</a>\n   268→        <a href=\"#technical-insights\">Technical Insights</a>\n   269→    </div>\n   270→\n   271→    <main class=\"container\">\n   272→        <div class=\"three-column-layout\">\n   273→            <section id=\"new-products\">\n   274→                <h2>New Products</h2>\n   275→\n   276→                <div class=\"item\">\n   277→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   278→                        <a href=\"https://antigravity.google/\">Google Antigravity</a>\n   279→                    </div>\n   280→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   281→                        Google's agent-first AI IDE and agentic development platform that coordinates multiple AI agents across editor, terminal, and browser. Features autonomous development with R.A.P.S Framework for parallel agent management and deep Google Cloud integration.\n   282→                    </div>\n   283→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   284→                        <span class=\"tag\">AI IDE</span>\n   285→                        <span class=\"tag\">Released: 2026-04-16</span>\n   286→                    </div>\n   287→                    <div class=\"quote\">\n   288→                        \"Agent-first approach with multi-agent coordination system\" - Google I/O 2026\n   289→                    </div>\n   290→                </div>\n   291→\n   292→            </section>\n   293→\n   294→            <section id=\"whitelist-updates\">\n   295→                <h2>Whitelist Updates</h2>\n   296→\n   297→                <div class=\"item\">\n   298→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   299→                        <a href=\"https://cursor.sh/changelog\">Cursor: Background Agents in Slack Integration</a>\n   300→                    </div>\n   301→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   302→                        <span class=\"update-type\">New Feature</span>\n   303→                        <span>2026-04-28</span>\n   304→                    </div>\n   305→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   306→                        You can now launch Background Agents directly from Slack by mentioning @Cursor. Agents can read the thread, understand context, and create PRs in GitHub without leaving the conversation.\n   307→                    </div>\n   308→                </div>\n   309→\n   310→                <div class=\"item\">\n   311→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   312→                        <a href=\"https://windsurf.com/changelog\">Windsurf: Devin for Terminal CLI</a>\n   313→                    </div>\n   314→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   315→                        <span class=\"update-type\">New Feature</span>\n   316→                        <span>2026-04-28</span>\n   317→                    </div>\n   318→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   319→                        Devin is now available for Terminal - a new CLI agent that runs on your machine with full access to your codebase, tools, and environment. Features seamless handoff to Devin in the cloud with its own VM, testing, video recordings, and autofix. Multi-model support including Opus 4.7, GPT-5.5, and SWE-1.6. Written in Rust for high performance.\n   320→                    </div>\n   321→                </div>\n   322→\n   323→                <div class=\"item\">\n   324→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   325→                        <a href=\"https://windsurf.com/changelog\">Windsurf: GPT-5.5 Model Support</a>\n   326→                    </div>\n   327→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   328→                        <span class=\"update-type\">Improvement</span>\n   329→                        <span>2026-04-24</span>\n   330→                    </div>\n   331→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   332→                        GPT-5.5 is now available in Windsurf IDE.\n   333→                    </div>\n   334→                    <div class=\"quote\">\n   335→                        \"Described as 'the most intelligent public model' with significant speed improvements for coding tasks\" - HackerNews\n   336→                    </div>\n   337→                </div>\n   338→\n   339→                <div class=\"item\">\n   340→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   341→                        <a href=\"https://github.blog/changelog/\">GitHub Copilot: GPT-5.5 Generally Available</a>\n   342→                    </div>\n   343→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   344→                        <span class=\"update-type\">Improvement</span>\n   345→                        <span>2026-04-24</span>\n   346→                    </div>\n   347→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   348→                        GPT-5.5 is now generally available for GitHub Copilot.\n   349→                    </div>\n   350→                    <div class=\"quote\">\n   351→                        \"Pricing at $5 per 1M input tokens and $30 per 1M output tokens\" - HackerNews\n   352→                    </div>\n   353→                </div>\n   354→\n   355→                <div class=\"item\">\n   356→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   357→                        <a href=\"https://cursor.sh/changelog\">Cursor 1.0 with BugBot and Background Agent</a>\n   358→                    </div>\n   359→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   360→                        <span class=\"update-type\">Major Release</span>\n   361→                        <span>2026-04-20</span>\n   362→                    </div>\n   363→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   364→                        Cursor 1.0 release featuring BugBot for automatic code review, Background Agent access for all users, Jupyter Notebook support, Memories feature, MCP one-click install, OAuth support, and richer chat responses with visualizations.\n   365→                    </div>\n   366→                </div>\n   367→\n   368→                <div class=\"item\">\n   369→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   370→                        <a href=\"https://windsurf.com/changelog\">Windsurf: Claude Opus 4.7 Model Support</a>\n   371→                    </div>\n   372→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   373→                        <span class=\"update-type\">Improvement</span>\n   374→                        <span>2026-04-16</span>\n   375→                    </div>\n   376→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   377→                        Claude Opus 4.7 is now available in Windsurf.\n   378→                    </div>\n   379→                </div>\n   380→\n   381→                <div class=\"item\">\n   382→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   383→                        <a href=\"https://github.blog/changelog/\">GitHub Copilot: Claude Opus 4.7 Generally Available</a>\n   384→                    </div>\n   385→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   386→                        <span class=\"update-type\">Improvement</span>\n   387→                        <span>2026-04-16</span>\n   388→                    </div>\n   389→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   390→                        Claude Opus 4.7 is now generally available for GitHub Copilot.\n   391→                    </div>\n   392→                </div>\n   393→\n   394→                <div class=\"item\">\n   395→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   396→                        <a href=\"https://github.blog/changelog/\">GitHub Copilot: Data Residency and FedRAMP Models Available</a>\n   397→                    </div>\n   398→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   399→                        <span class=\"update-type\">Improvement</span>\n   400→                        <span>2026-04-13</span>\n   401→                    </div>\n   402→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   403→                        Data residency (US + EU) and FedRAMP-authorized models are now available in Copilot for enterprise and government customers.\n   404→                    </div>\n   405→                </div>\n   406→\n   407→            </section>\n   408→\n   409→            <section id=\"technical-insights\">\n   410→                <h2>Technical Insights</h2>\n   411→\n   412→                <div class=\"item\">\n   413→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   414→                        <span class=\"insight-type news\">News</span>\n   415→                        <a href=\"https://www.theverge.com/2026/4/27/24254919/china-blocks-meta-manus-acquisition-2-billion\">China Blocks Meta's $2B Acquisition of Manus</a>\n   416→                    </div>\n   417→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">Robert Hart · TechMedia · 2026-04-27</div>\n   418→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   419→                        China's economic watchdog blocked Meta's $2 billion acquisition of AI agent startup Manus. The deal was largely complete and Manus was already integrated into some of Meta's tools, but Beijing canceled the acquisition without explaining their decision.\n   420→                    </div>\n   421→                </div>\n   422→\n   423→                <div class=\"item\">\n   424→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   425→                        <span class=\"insight-type news\">News</span>\n   426→                        <a href=\"https://www.theverge.com/2026/4/27/24255129/elon-musk-drops-fraud-claims-openai-sam-altman-trial\">Musk Drops Fraud Claims Against OpenAI</a>\n   427→                    </div>\n   428→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">Robert Hart · TechMedia · 2026-04-27</div>\n   429→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   430→                        Elon Musk voluntarily dropped fraud claims against OpenAI and Sam Altman before the trial began. The federal judge granted Musk's request to streamline the case, focusing instead on ensuring OpenAI adheres to its public charitable mission.\n   431→                    </div>\n   432→                </div>\n   433→\n   434→                <div class=\"item\">\n   435→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   436→                        <span class=\"insight-type news\">News</span>\n   437→                        <a href=\"https://www.theverge.com/2026/4/27/24248441/microsoft-openai-agi-agreement-dead\">Microsoft-OpenAI AGI Agreement Terminated</a>\n   438→                    </div>\n   439→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">Elizabeth Lopatto · TechMedia · 2026-04-27</div>\n   440→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   441→                        The landmark AGI agreement between Microsoft and OpenAI has been terminated, fundamentally changing their partnership structure. The original agreement gave Microsoft exclusive rights to OpenAI's technology until AGI was achieved.\n   442→                    </div>\n   443→                </div>\n   444→\n   445→                <div class=\"item\">\n   446→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   447→                        <span class=\"insight-type news\">News</span>\n   448→                        <a href=\"https://www.theverge.com/2026/4/27/24251890/ai-designed-car-shape\">AI-Designed Car is Taking Shape</a>\n   449→                    </div>\n   450→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">Robert Hart · TechMedia · 2026-04-27</div>\n   451→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   452→                        A vehicle designed entirely by AI is moving from concept to reality. The article showcases images of the AI-designed car, highlighting how generative AI is revolutionizing automotive design and engineering.\n   453→                    </div>\n   454→                </div>\n   455→\n   456→                <div class=\"item\">\n   457→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   458→                        <span class=\"insight-type investigation\">Investigation</span>\n   459→                        <a href=\"https://www.theverge.com/2026/4/25/24243497/openai-super-pac-fake-news-site-ai-reporters\">OpenAI's Super PAC Funds AI-Staffed 'News' Site</a>\n   460→                    </div>\n   461→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">Terrence O'Brien · TechMedia · 2026-04-25</div>\n   462→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   463→                        Investigation reveals that OpenAI's super PAC appears to be funding The Wire by Acutus, a suspiciously pro-AI news site where most 'reporters' appear to be bots. One reporter, Michael Chen, likely doesn't exist.\n   464→                    </div>\n   465→                </div>\n   466→\n   467→                <div class=\"item\">\n   468→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   469→                        <span class=\"insight-type news\">News</span>\n   470→                        <a href=\"https://www.theverge.com/2026/4/25/24252153/sam-altman-apology-tumbler-ridge-school-shooting-chatgpt\">Altman Apologizes to Town After ChatGPT Incident</a>\n   471→                    </div>\n   472→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">Jay Peters · TechMedia · 2026-04-25</div>\n   473→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   474→                        Sam Altman personally apologized to the Canadian town of Tumbler Ridge after a school shooting suspect described violent scenarios to ChatGPT. While OpenAI banned the account, they did not alert law enforcement about the person.\n   475→                    </div>\n   476→                </div>\n   477→\n   478→                <div class=\"item\">\n   479→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   480→                        <span class=\"insight-type opinion\">Opinion</span>\n   481→                        <a href=\"https://www.theverge.com/2026/4/25/24251899/sinceerly-ai-extension-makes-ai-writing-sound-human\">Sinceerly: Anti-Grammarly for AI Writing</a>\n   482→                    </div>\n   483→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">Terrence O'Brien · TechMedia · 2026-04-25</div>\n   484→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   485→                        A satirical Chrome extension called Sinceerly promises to banish AI writing patterns like the em-dash, remove phrases that are dead giveaways of AI, and even introduce typos. Developer Dan Horwitz calls it an 'anti-Grammarly' commentary on AI detection.\n   486→                    </div>\n   487→                </div>\n   488→\n   489→                <div class=\"item\">\n   490→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   491→                        <span class=\"insight-type news\">News</span>\n   492→                        <a href=\"https://www.theverge.com/2026/4/25/24251463/suno-android-auto-carplay-ai-music\">Suno AI Music on Android Auto</a>\n   493→                    </div>\n   494→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">Terrence O'Brien · TechMedia · 2026-04-25</div>\n   495→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   496→                        AI music generation platform Suno is now available on Android Auto, with CarPlay support in development. The integration currently supports search, library, explore, and playback control, allowing users to stream AI-generated music while driving.\n   497→                    </div>\n   498→                </div>\n   499→\n   500→                <div class=\"item\">\n   501→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   502→                        <span class=\"insight-type technical\">Technical</span>\n   503→                        <a href=\"https://medium.com/@visrow/the-biggest-ai-trends-and-tools-emerging-in-april-2026-8a491e6d546f\">Biggest AI Trends Emerging in April 2026</a>\n   504→                    </div>\n   505→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">Vishal Mysore · Platform · 2026-04-24</div>\n   506→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   507→                        AI is evolving beyond chatbots into autonomous execution systems. Key trends include coding agents becoming full software engineers, TurboQuant KV cache compression achieving 6x memory reduction, multi-agent systems replacing linear workflows, and AI runtime layers emerging as new infrastructure category.\n   508→                    </div>\n   509→                </div>\n   510→\n   511→                <div class=\"item\">\n   512→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   513→                        <span class=\"insight-type technical\">Technical</span>\n   514→                        <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/24/deepseek-v4/\">DeepSeek V4 Analysis</a>\n   515→                    </div>\n   516→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">Simon Willison · Blog · 2026-04-24</div>\n   517→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   518→                        Chinese AI lab DeepSeek released V4-Pro and V4-Flash preview models. Simon Willison provides analysis of their performance and pricing positioning relative to frontier models.\n   519→                    </div>\n   520→                </div>\n   521→\n   522→                <div class=\"item\">\n   523→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   524→                        <span class=\"insight-type technical\">Technical</span>\n   525→                        <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/24/honker/\">honker: Postgres NOTIFY/LISTEN for SQLite</a>\n   526→                    </div>\n   527→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">Simon Willison · Blog · 2026-04-24</div>\n   528→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   529→                        Rust SQLite extension implementing Postgres-style queue and stream functionality with transactional outbox pattern. Enables Kafka-style durable streams and queue operations in SQLite.\n   530→                    </div>\n   531→                </div>\n   532→\n   533→                <div class=\"item\">\n   534→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   535→                        <span class=\"insight-type opinion\">Opinion</span>\n   536→                        <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/24/claude-code-quality/\">Claude Code Quality Report Update</a>\n   537→                    </div>\n   538→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">Simon Willison · Blog · 2026-04-24</div>\n   539→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   540→                        Anthropic released a postmortem on Claude Code quality issues over the past two months. Key bug: clearing thinking from idle sessions kept happening every turn instead of once, causing forgetful behavior. Important lessons for agentic system builders.\n   541→                    </div>\n   542→                </div>\n   543→\n   544→                <div class=\"item\">\n   545→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   546→                        <span class=\"insight-type technical\">Technical</span>\n   547→                        <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/24/for-you-feed/\">Bluesky's For You Feed Architecture</a>\n   548→                    </div>\n   549→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">Simon Willison · Blog · 2026-04-24</div>\n   550→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   551→                        Deep dive into how spacecowboy's Bluesky feed serves 72,000 users from a single Go process with SQLite on a gaming PC (16 cores, 96GB RAM, 4TB NVMe). Total cost: $30/month. Stores 90 days of data in 419GB SQLite database.\n   552→                    </div>\n   553→                </div>\n   554→\n   555→                <div class=\"item\">\n   556→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   557→                        <span class=\"insight-type tutorial\">Tutorial</span>\n   558→                        <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/23/liteparse-web/\">Extract PDF Text in Browser with LiteParse</a>\n   559→                    </div>\n   560→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">Simon Willison · Blog · 2026-04-23</div>\n   561→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   562→                        Simon ported LlamaIndex's LiteParse PDF extraction tool to run entirely in the browser using the same libraries as the Node.js version.\n   563→                    </div>\n   564→                </div>\n   565→\n   566→                <div class=\"item\">\n   567→                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n   568→                        <span class=\"insight-type opinion\">Opinion</span>\n   569→                        <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/23/gpt-5.5-pelican/\">GPT-5.5 Pelican Generation Test</a>\n   570→                    </div>\n   571→                    <div class=\"item-meta\">Simon Willison · Blog · 2026-04-23</div>\n   572→                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n   573→                        Testing GPT-5.5's image generation capabilities with the classic 'pelican riding a bicycle' prompt. Found it to be fast, effective and highly capable.\n   574→                    </div>\n   575→                </div>\n   576→\n   577→            </section>\n   578→        </div>\n   579→    </main>\n   580→\n   581→    <footer>\n   582→        <p>\n   583→            <a href=\"#top\">Back to top</a> ·\n   584→            Generated on April 30, 2026 ·\n   585→            <a href=\"https://agentdaily.com\">Agentic Coding Research</a>\n   586→        </p>\n   587→    </footer>\n   588→</body>\n   589→</html>\n   590→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"
      }
    ]
